      <div class="planting" id="planting">
            <div class="text-plant">
                  <h2 class="subheading">PLANTING MAIZE</h2>
                  <p> Land preparation
                        In the southern parts of the country where vegetation cover is fairly dense, land clearing
                        should be carried out well ahead of the rains. Make ridges as soon as the rains start. Although
                        the crop benefits from the deep cultivation, maize yields are not reduced when zero tillage is
                        adopted. For high grain yield, sow maize in well prepared ridges of 75cm apart. Because the
                        soils in the savannas are loose, animal drawn implements can be used for preparing ridges or by
                        had hoe and tractor mounted tillage equipment.
                  </p>
                  <br>

                  <p>Planting date
                        Plant as soon as the rains are established, preferably immediately after a good rain. The
                        establishment of the rains varies according to the different ecological zones and from one year
                        to another. However, optimum planting date in the major ecological zones usually lies within the
                        following ranges:
                        i. Sudan Savanna: third weeks of June to early July
                        ii. Northern Guinea Savanna: Last week in May 1st to 2 nd week in June
                        iii. Southern Guinea Savanna: Mid-May to second week in June
                        iv. Forest Zone: Late February March for the first season and late July-August for the second
                        season. Plant extra or early maturing varieties by late July and early August in order to allow
                        maturity time to coincide with on-set of dry season.

                  </p>
            </div>
      </div>

